description:
Two everyday jobs when reading C++ binaries (lesson 2.05). Memory is simulated as a map from 8-aligned addresses to 8-byte values:

```cpp
using Memory = std::map<std::uint64_t, std::uint64_t>;

int slotOf(std::uint32_t displacement);
std::optional<std::uint64_t> virtualTarget(const Memory& m, std::uint64_t object, std::uint32_t displacement);
std::optional<std::string> classOf(const Memory& m, const std::map<std::uint64_t, std::string>& locators, std::uint64_t object);
std::string undecorate(const std::string& name);
```

- **slotOf** turns the displacement in `call qword ptr [rax+disp]` into a slot number (`0x18` is slot 3).
- **virtualTarget** does what a virtual call does: read the vtable pointer at `object`, then the function address at `vtable + displacement`. Any missing value, or a vtable pointer of 0, gives `std::nullopt`.
- **classOf** reads the vtable pointer at `object`, then the locator pointer stored just before the table (at `vtable - 8`), and looks that locator up in `locators` to get the class name.
- **undecorate** turns an MSVC decorated name into its qualified name:
  - `?name@Scope1@Scope2@@...` becomes `Scope2::Scope1::name` (the parts after `?` up to `@@` are the name, then scopes innermost first; everything after `@@` is ignored).
  - `??0Class@...@@...` is a constructor: `...::Class::Class`. `??1Class@...@@...` is a destructor: `...::Class::~Class`.
  - A name not starting with `?` is returned unchanged.

